{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>יערף [may it drip].</b> From the same root as יערף מזבחותם [may their altars drip], in the sense of descending." "<b>לקחי [my teaching].</b> That which one takes from it, in the sense of למוד [learning], and likewise כי לקח טוב [for a good teaching]." "<b>כשעירם [like showers].</b> The correct interpretation in my eyes is that they are fine [droplets]." "<b>וכרביבים [and like raindrops].</b> From the root רב, and both are adjectives. The proof is that עשב [grass] is stronger than דשא [tender grass]. Some say that שעירים [showers] is from the root שער [hair], and others said from the root of סערת ה׳ [the storm of YHWH]; and if it is with a sin, likewise וישתער עליו [he stormed upon him], like ויסתער עליו [he rushed upon him]. It is also possible that it has no parallel in Scripture. Now the meaning is doubled, as is the way of all prophecies, for emphasis. And the essential meaning is that Moses prayed that his words would be like dew and like rain, that they not return empty, but rather saturate the earth, for so it is written. And the meaning is that his words enter the hearts of the hearers like the action of rain upon the earth, to cause it to give birth and to sprout."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {3 "Resolved י״א as יש אומרים ('some say'); the phrase סערת ה׳ is a truncated citation of an expression involving a storm of YHWH."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:25:50.457224101Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}